WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.419
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.860
VEST: Today, as we honor and
as we learn from Kofi Annan,

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:21.190
we take great pride
in the small role

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.580
that we played in his
education and in shaping

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:26.680
his early career.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:30.820
But we also bear witness
to the importance

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:34.600
of maintaining the openness
of our universities

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:38.350
to foreign students,
scholars, and faculty.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.010
We must guard, even in
these complicated times,

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:47.650
and protect this fundamental
American and academic value

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:50.120
and principle.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.780
It is my privilege
and honor, once again,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.800
to welcome to MIT the
Secretary-General of the United

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.500
Nations, His
Excellency, Kofi Annan.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.946
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:18.641 --> 00:01:19.766
ANNAN: Thank you very much.

00:01:19.766 --> 00:01:23.718
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:23.718 --> 00:01:25.200
Thank you very much.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.320
Thank you very much,
my dear friends.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.870
President Vest, let me thank
you for those very warm and kind

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:46.530
words of introduction.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.650
And Dean Schmalensee, I'm
really happy that I'm back here

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.220
with all of you at Sloan.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:02:01.050
Distinguished faculty members
and students and dear friends,

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.880
it is indeed a pleasure
for me to be back here

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.449
and to join you today for
this anniversary celebration.

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:11.730
Over the course
of half a century,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.240
Sloan School of Management
has built itself

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.640
into one of the world's
academic powerhouses.

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.690
I say world and not just
country because right

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.570
from the beginning,
Sloan looked well

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:28.865
beyond the confines
of this campus

00:02:28.865 --> 00:02:31.820
and encouraged people
from many nations,

00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:35.180
as we heard from the
president, to study here

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.660
and was eager to
find and advance

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.450
the cause of
international cooperation,

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.970
scholarly and otherwise.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.660
Congratulations
on this milestone.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.650
Three decades ago, I was
fortunate myself to become

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.050
part of the Sloan community.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:58.520
And it is good to see so many
familiar faces since I've

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:03.150
been down here yesterday.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:06.420
And an event like this
really brings back

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.890
memories of when I first came
here, what the place was like,

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:17.120
and where I myself was at
at that stage of my life.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.440
I recall particular
that Sloan exposed me

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.040
to some very interesting work
in organizational culture

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.460
and psychology and
how to manage change.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.610
That may sound like
jargon to most of you,

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:34.710
but I can assure
you that it came in

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.770
very handy at the United
Nations when I needed.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:45.510
Back in 1982 when I was working
with the High Commissioner

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:48.000
for Refugees in
Geneva and we were

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.720
trying to grapple with the
crisis of the boat people,

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.380
I invited my good
friend Professor Schein

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:58.580
to come to Geneva
to help us improve

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:05.810
our internal communication
and to review our mission.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.610
When we got there
and everybody came,

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.693
we thought we were going
to have a straightforward

00:04:09.693 --> 00:04:11.420
discussion for one hour.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.080
Three hours later, we were
all physically and emotionally

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:19.250
drained from what had
quickly turned out

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.160
to be a frank soul-searching
exercise for all of us

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:28.880
what it meant to be working for
the United Nations, what were

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.900
we trying to do with the
refugee crisis of the time,

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.990
and how best to get
different people

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:39.140
and people from nationalities
around the world

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.660
to work better together.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.870
Some frustrations had been
bottled up obviously for years.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.710
But once the
floodgates were open,

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:51.980
we found new ways forward,
and a truly new sense

00:04:51.980 --> 00:04:54.710
of unity and purpose emerged.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.860
This session was so successful
that I organized another one

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:03.350
in 1990 where Ed Schein
and Lester Thurow came

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.180
to talk to the group.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:07.940
We gathered about
30 senior officials

00:05:07.940 --> 00:05:12.230
from 26 different countries
to discuss similar issues,

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:16.250
and they all walked
away very enthusiastic.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.040
I like to think that
we are replicating

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:23.810
that exercise on a global
level among peoples and nations

00:05:23.810 --> 00:05:27.740
as we strive to build
the trust, confidence,

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.610
and a sense of shared values
and responsibility needed

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:36.990
to address the urgent issues
and threats of our time.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:40.220
We are all aware that
more and more challenges

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:44.300
from environmental degradation
to drug trafficking

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:48.290
and the spread of
diseases such as HIV/AIDS

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:52.440
have a global dimension.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.090
Through work, travel,
and trips to the store,

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:00.360
we can all see that
trade and communications

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.110
are stitching human family
ever more closely together.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:13.190
These phenomena have also
have to make the 21st century

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:17.930
a very troubling times
for our global village.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:20.480
Distrust between
cultures and religion

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:23.090
often leads to
violence and has been

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:28.710
aggravated by the
terrorist attacks of 9/11.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:34.020
Concern is mounting because
of global economic uncertainty

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.020
and because the benefits
of globalization

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.160
have been shared so unevenly.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.360
Confidence in markets has
been dealt a further blow with

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.930
a series of corporate
scandals in the United States

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:53.940
and the gathering feeling that
markets by themselves cannot

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.320
respond to the real needs of
society or provide the public

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.500
goods that humankind
needs to survive.

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:07.460
In an age of interdependence,
global citizenship

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.370
is a crucial pillar
for progress.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:13.190
In a series of global
meetings and conferences

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:16.380
over the past two
years in particular,

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:19.770
world leaders have
tried to define

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:22.630
what that citizenship means.

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:26.140
They have been trying to build
an inclusive, responsive,

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:31.270
effective, international
system from which

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:34.450
all people can benefit
from and in which all

00:07:34.450 --> 00:07:37.840
feel they have a stake.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:40.120
Shared responsibility
was at the heart

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.880
of the declaration
adopted at the Millennium

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.670
Summit in September 2000.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:48.940
160 heads of states
and government

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:51.280
gathered in New York.

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:54.220
All countries came
together not just

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:57.250
to express their general hopes
for peace and development

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:01.750
in the 21st century but
also to give their backing,

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.960
their backing to a set of
very specific, time-bound

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.550
objectives, which have since
become known as the Millennium

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.980
Development Goals.

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:16.960
The goals include reducing
hunger, providing access

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:19.900
to safe drinking
water, and ensuring

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.930
universal primary education.

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:26.290
They will be closely
monitored and measured--

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:30.340
how many kids are in
school, how quickly

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:34.090
hunger and extreme
poverty are being reduced.

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:37.360
And we will advertise
the results in such a way

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:39.880
that we hope will
galvanize politics

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.120
and policymaking so
that the goals can be

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.680
met by the target date of 2015.

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:54.200
Governments faced the first
test of their commitment

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:59.300
to these goals last November
at the World Trade Organization

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:01.310
meeting in Doha.

00:09:01.310 --> 00:09:05.880
There, trust was the
main issue on the table--

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:09.030
trust, because the
developing countries

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:13.960
have heard a lot of talk
about free and fair trade

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:18.130
but seen too little,
far too little, of it.

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:22.530
They want to know that
their products will

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.740
have an equal chance to
compete in the global market.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.860
That chance is
currently denied them

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:34.950
because of tariffs
on their goods

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:38.130
and because of subsidies
given to their competitors

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:39.930
in the rich countries--

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:42.930
subsidies that I might
add also perpetuate

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:47.100
unsustainable practices in
farming, transport, and energy

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:48.630
use.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:52.410
The new round of negotiations
agreed to at Doha

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:57.090
offers the prospect that
markets will truly be open.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:09:59.010
But it is only a promise.

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:02.520
And it is too soon to
say whether that trust

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:06.930
in the trading system
will be achieved.

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:08.670
Only time will
tell, but I think we

00:10:08.670 --> 00:10:13.890
need to work hard and press
ahead to open the markets truly

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.050
for all countries.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.930
Next came the Monterrey
Conference on financing

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.350
for development last March.

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.900
That conference was
also an exercise

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:28.440
in recognizing shared
responsibilities.

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.740
The conference generated
substantial new pledges

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:34.350
for official
development assistance,

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:39.360
reversing a decade-long
decline, and made good progress

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:44.520
on issues such as debt relief,
investment, and corruption.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:49.350
Just as important, developed
and developing countries

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:51.600
reached a common
understanding on

00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:54.150
their respective
responsibilities

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:58.660
in the pursuit of balanced
and equitable development.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.080
And finally, last month
at the World Summit

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:04.590
on Sustainable Development
in Johannesburg,

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:08.580
global citizenship once
again took center stage.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:12.270
All leaders committed themselves
to a path of development

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:14.850
and economic growth
that safeguards

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:20.070
resources and ecosystems
for succeeding generations.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.710
Leaders from rich
countries in particular

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:27.570
agreed to reduce their
nations' ecological footprint

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.850
on the planet.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:33.810
Taken together, these
summits and conferences

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:37.500
give us a blueprint, a
blueprint for development

00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:42.420
that puts people, not
states, not GDP statistics,

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.660
at the center of policy making.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:49.500
The overriding challenge
now is implementation.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:53.520
And for that, we shall need
people from all sectors--

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.230
the public sector,
private sector,

00:11:56.230 --> 00:12:00.360
civil society to forge more
and better partnerships

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.000
to implement these schemes.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:06.570
One of the most welcome
developments at the United

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:11.700
Nations in recent years has been
the steadily growing engagement

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:15.930
of the business community
both in policy forums

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:19.620
and in projects on the ground.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:23.880
Though the relationship is
not without its difficulties,

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.790
there is growing
recognition that we

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:31.290
must move beyond the
politics of confrontation

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:35.250
and that solutions to poverty,
environmental degradation,

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.460
and other challenges
can only be found

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.010
if the private sector
is involved and is

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:43.470
an effective partner.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:45.940
More and more
businesses themselves

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:50.760
are coming to recognize how they
depend on international norms

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:55.080
and standards for the conduct
of business on a global scale

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.770
and that the United Nations
wide-ranging work in peace

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:02.510
and development has an
impact on how they do.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:09.030
In 1999, at the World
Economic Forum in Davos,

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:11.745
I introduced the idea
of a Global Compact.

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:17.560
The Global Compact
was based on my belief

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:22.940
that markets and human
well-being can go hand-in-hand.

00:13:22.940 --> 00:13:25.970
Over the long run,
human well-being

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:31.500
can be dramatically advanced
by well-functioning markets.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:38.330
But markets themselves cannot be
sustained if they do not ensure

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:41.840
human well-being.

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.820
I asked businesses at
that time to embrace

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:49.320
nine universal principles in
the areas of human rights,

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:52.890
core labor standards,
and the environment

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:56.490
and to enact these principles
within their spheres

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:01.120
of influence and make it part
of their way of doing business.

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:04.500
I picked these
ideas because I was

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:10.660
worried by a severe imbalance
in global rule making.

00:14:10.660 --> 00:14:14.080
While there are extensive
and enforceable rules

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:18.580
for economic priorities such as
intellectual property rights,

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:22.990
there are few strong measures
for equally vital concerns

00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:27.010
such as human rights
and the environment.

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:32.440
The compact has since become
more than a call to action.

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.770
Today, it involves not
only businesses but also

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:40.310
labor federations and
nongovernmental organizations.

00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:43.450
It has promoted the
importance of universal values

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:47.440
and encouraged investors to
look harder at opportunities

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.870
in the least developed
countries, particularly

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:51.730
in Africa.

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:56.490
The compact has created
a learning forum,

00:14:56.490 --> 00:15:01.630
a worldwide academic network
that examines case studies,

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:05.580
trying to determine what
works and what doesn't.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:09.700
And I am pleased that Sloan
plays an important role

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.890
in this forum.

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:14.320
None of this is
meant to is meant

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:18.370
as a substitute for
action by governments

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:22.870
or as a regulatory framework
or code of conduct.

00:15:22.870 --> 00:15:26.020
Rather, the compact is
a voluntary initiative,

00:15:26.020 --> 00:15:28.120
a platform for
showing how markets

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:32.860
can be made to serve the
needs of society as a whole.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:38.980
Business may ask why they should
go down this path, especially

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:42.610
if it involves taking steps
that the competition might

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:47.680
not or steps they
feel are rightly

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:50.510
the province of governments.

00:15:50.510 --> 00:15:55.290
Sometimes doing what
is right, for example,

00:15:55.290 --> 00:15:59.150
[? equity ?] [? efficiency ?]
or creating decent workplace

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:04.790
conditions, is in the
immediate interest of business.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:09.200
Sometimes we must do
what is right simply

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:13.160
because not to do
so would be wrong.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:16.610
And sometimes we do what
is right to help usher

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:23.030
in a new day of new
reforms and new behaviors.

00:16:23.030 --> 00:16:26.390
We do not want business
to do anything different

00:16:26.390 --> 00:16:29.300
from their normal business.

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:33.290
We one business to do
their business differently.

00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:38.230
We want them to
continue what they

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:43.360
do but bear in mind these
values that touch all of us.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:48.520
And dear friends, openness
is the emerging hallmark

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:50.930
of our time.

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:53.780
But we need to make it work.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:56.110
Otherwise, countries
and peoples might

00:16:56.110 --> 00:17:01.030
retreat behind protectionism,
or worst of all,

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:04.359
reject global citizenship
or globalization

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:09.520
in favor of narrow concepts
of national interest

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:12.609
not at all appropriate
for this interdependent

00:17:12.609 --> 00:17:16.089
world we live in, for
this global village.

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:20.079
Business is well-placed not
only to generate employment,

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:23.680
investment, and growth
but also to advance

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:26.630
the global citizenship.

00:17:26.630 --> 00:17:31.920
Sloan is well-placed to
teach more than economics,

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:35.960
accounting, and finance and
to help define the parameters

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:38.240
of corporate citizenship.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.990
And I am delighted to hear
that your program is already

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:44.020
evolving in that
direction, and there

00:17:44.020 --> 00:17:48.650
is a great deal of
enthusiasm for the program

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.900
on corporate citizenship.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:56.100
And the United Nations is
well-placed to promote dialogue

00:17:56.100 --> 00:18:00.360
that will build trust and
create the multilateral norms

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:06.030
and frameworks needed to fulfill
our shared responsibilities.

00:18:06.030 --> 00:18:10.740
All of us, the private sector,
civil society, labor unions,

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:14.430
NGOs, universities and
foundations, and individuals

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:20.160
like you must come together
in an alliance for progress.

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:23.460
Together, we can and
must move from value

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:27.900
to values, from shareholders
to stakeholders,

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.680
and from balance sheets
to balanced development.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:35.670
Together, we can and must face
the dangers ahead and bring

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:38.430
solutions within reach.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:42.140
Thank you very much,
and congratulations

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:46.043
to the Sloan community
on this important day.

00:18:46.043 --> 00:18:47.835
I will now be happy to
take your questions.

00:18:47.835 --> 00:18:52.614
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:04.518
Thank you very much.

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.900
PRESENTER: Thank you,
Mr. Secretary-General.

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:20.010
I have a number of
questions from the audience,

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:22.860
and perhaps more
will be forthcoming.

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:26.870
But let me pose one for you.

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:29.840
Do you see a link between
corporate responsibility

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:32.390
and corporate citizenship
on the one hand

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:35.590
and the gulf between haves
and have-nots on the other?

00:19:35.590 --> 00:19:37.340
Do companies have
a positive role

00:19:37.340 --> 00:19:39.560
to play in creating
a sustainable future?

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:47.930
ANNAN: I think from what I have
said this morning it is clear

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:52.440
that I see a very important
role for the corporate world.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:57.290
And I think corporations
that respond and react

00:19:57.290 --> 00:20:01.640
to these norms do
make a difference

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:03.500
in the communities
and the societies

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:06.140
in which they operate.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:09.290
Corporations and the
leaders of corporations

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:11.450
are often very
important personalities

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:15.500
in their own communities and can
influence policy and decision

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:18.350
making by governments
and help steer laws

00:20:18.350 --> 00:20:20.330
in the right direction.

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:23.180
But they also make a difference
in the day-to-day lives

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:25.340
of the individual.

00:20:25.340 --> 00:20:28.940
Corporations don't have
to wait for governments

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:34.400
to pass laws for them to know
that their productions should

00:20:34.400 --> 00:20:39.980
not pollute the water that
produces fish for the village.

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:44.270
They need not wait
for governments

00:20:44.270 --> 00:20:45.920
to pay a decent wage.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:48.590
They need not wait
for governments

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:52.280
to try and train their
people and give them--

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:54.470
take the question of HIV/AIDS.

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:58.190
Corporations have done fantastic
things around the world.

00:20:58.190 --> 00:21:01.280
Volkswagen [? to ?]
Brazil, for years,

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:04.850
watched its good managers
contract the disease

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:05.757
and die off.

00:21:05.757 --> 00:21:07.340
And it took the
decision, we are going

00:21:07.340 --> 00:21:09.170
to do something about this.

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:12.530
Came up with a program of
education and treatment

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:15.680
for their managers
and their families

00:21:15.680 --> 00:21:20.570
and then begin to see these
creative men and women live on,

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:23.210
continue to deliver
to their company,

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:27.320
and passing the message onto
their families and communities

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:28.970
how to protect themselves.

00:21:28.970 --> 00:21:35.180
So the role of corporations
in the issue of development

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:38.600
and their society is
extremely important.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:41.460
And some are doing extremely
well [? nowadays ?] [? at ?]

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:44.090
[? this ?] to look at what
works and what doesn't.

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:46.580
And corporations do have a role.

00:21:46.580 --> 00:21:47.960
PRESENTER: Thank you.

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:49.640
Another question
from the audience

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:54.410
is, what can we at MIT,
at Sloan, and those of us

00:21:54.410 --> 00:21:59.420
assembled do in particular
to help realize or advance

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:02.630
the Global Compact Initiative?

00:22:02.630 --> 00:22:06.800
ANNAN: I think you're already
on the right track by training

00:22:06.800 --> 00:22:09.770
your students and discussing
the Global Compact

00:22:09.770 --> 00:22:11.360
Initiative with them.

00:22:11.360 --> 00:22:14.090
And I'm sure you
also have a chance

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:17.000
to advise many
corporations, and you

00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:21.450
would be able to expose them to
this and talk to them about it.

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:25.220
I've had the chance of meeting
some of the Sloan Fellows

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:27.110
in New York, and
I've been struck

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:29.340
by how enthusiastic they are.

00:22:29.340 --> 00:22:32.810
And I know that quite a
few of your professors

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:35.540
are also working on
the Global Compact.

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:38.900
And so I think apart
from teaching it here,

00:22:38.900 --> 00:22:41.270
by the learning
forum, and what we

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:44.750
are going to put on the
web to share with others

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:49.975
becomes an important way
of spreading the word.

00:22:49.975 --> 00:22:51.350
The other thing
that we are doing

00:22:51.350 --> 00:22:54.020
is a learning forums
that we've introduced

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:56.840
where, for example,
corporations can come together

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:00.080
to discuss what their
responsibilities are

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:03.380
and what their posture should
be if they find themselves

00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:06.210
operating in areas of conflict.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:07.910
What is expected
of corporations?

00:23:07.910 --> 00:23:09.890
Whom should they
turn to for advice?

00:23:09.890 --> 00:23:12.140
And how do they work
together to cope?

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:14.870
And all this, I think,
is going to be extremely

00:23:14.870 --> 00:23:17.330
helpful for corporations.

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:20.720
And I'm extremely happy
that US corporations are

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:22.670
beginning to join the compact.

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:25.130
We've had tremendous
response from Europe,

00:23:25.130 --> 00:23:28.790
from Latin America, Asia,
and elsewhere in Africa.

00:23:28.790 --> 00:23:31.520
Recently, Hewlett-Packard
has joined.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:33.470
Pfizer is coming on board.

00:23:33.470 --> 00:23:37.460
And I expect many
more will join.

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:39.290
PRESENTER: What
responsibilities do

00:23:39.290 --> 00:23:41.300
American corporations
in particular

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:44.630
have in the global community?

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:47.630
Do you see a special
responsibility because of--

00:23:47.630 --> 00:23:52.220
ANNAN: Well, I
think, today, it is

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:58.340
business and these corporations
that have become the main motor

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:00.260
for the creation of wealth.

00:24:00.260 --> 00:24:01.520
They have technology.

00:24:01.520 --> 00:24:02.390
They have money.

00:24:02.390 --> 00:24:04.110
And they have management.

00:24:04.110 --> 00:24:06.320
And they also have
a global reach.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:11.090
In some ways, a cooperation is
understood faster and better

00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:15.020
than governments that we
do live in a global world.

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:17.840
And they work across borders.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:20.930
And they also know
that in doing that you

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:23.460
need to have some rules.

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:28.460
And I think the corporations
that operate around the world

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:34.250
should see their role not only
as a New York-headquartered

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:38.870
or Silicon Valley-headquartered,
but they should try and also

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:43.610
lift the people in the countries
in which they are operating

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:46.880
and also help the
societies by passing

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:48.770
on some of the
knowledge and some

00:24:48.770 --> 00:24:51.440
of the technology
and the training.

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:55.190
Where they fail to do
this, they inadvertently

00:24:55.190 --> 00:24:59.060
become part of the
conflict or the problem.

00:24:59.060 --> 00:24:59.730
We've seen this.

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:01.140
I can give you an example.

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:06.790
Look at what is happening
in Nigeria with Shell, where

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:11.690
the villages around the
oil production sites

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:15.890
feel they have seen nothing
of the benefits that accrue

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:18.820
from exploitation of their oil.

00:25:18.820 --> 00:25:21.790
The money goes to the
central government.

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:23.810
The shareholders
take their profits,

00:25:23.810 --> 00:25:26.698
and they see nothing and
continue to live in squalor.

00:25:26.698 --> 00:25:28.490
I think it was two or
three months ago when

00:25:28.490 --> 00:25:32.100
a group of women demonstrated
and took over one of the site.

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.870
And now Shell has indicated
it is going to help them,

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:38.030
and it is going to
put some money back.

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:42.348
And in fact, even if it is not
direct responsibility of Shell,

00:25:42.348 --> 00:25:44.390
in a way, it could have
pressure the governments.

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:47.570
Said, don't you think we
should be doing something

00:25:47.570 --> 00:25:49.340
for the villages around here?

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.920
Can we pull together
and do something?

00:25:51.920 --> 00:25:56.130
These kinds of
gestures go a long way.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:58.340
And I know some
companies have done it.

00:25:58.340 --> 00:26:01.220
And I would urge others to
do that-- not only train

00:26:01.220 --> 00:26:03.470
the local people,
pass on technology,

00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:09.270
but ensure that we spread the
benefits a little bit more.

00:26:09.270 --> 00:26:11.960
PRESENTER: I have a
one-word question,

00:26:11.960 --> 00:26:13.850
which I think we'd
all be pleased

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:15.560
if you would address briefly.

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:16.790
And that question is--

00:26:16.790 --> 00:26:17.360
Iraq?

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:22.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:22.940 --> 00:26:25.500
ANNAN: I thought I was
going to get away without--

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:26.500
PRESENTER: Now, hold on.

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:27.338
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:27.338 --> 00:26:28.130
ANNAN: --answering.

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:31.302
PRESENTER: It's not a
perfect [? scream, ?] no.

00:26:31.302 --> 00:26:34.790
ANNAN: No, Iraq, obviously,
is a very topical issue.

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:38.360
And it's a very difficult
issue for all of us.

00:26:38.360 --> 00:26:41.930
And the UN has been involved
with this problem for over 12

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:44.660
years now.

00:26:44.660 --> 00:26:47.840
And the inspectors, as
you know, were withdrawn

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.150
from Iraq in December '98.

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:55.730
And since then, we've not
had inspectors back in Iraq.

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:57.470
Earlier this year,
I got a letter

00:26:57.470 --> 00:27:02.840
from the Secretary-General
of the Arab League

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:04.700
saying that Iraqis
would want to sit

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:07.670
with you to discuss the
return of inspectors

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:09.710
without conditions.

00:27:09.710 --> 00:27:11.750
So we arranged to meet.

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:14.960
That was, I think,
in February or March.

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:16.700
We met three times this year.

00:27:16.700 --> 00:27:19.850
The last meeting was in Vienna.

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:23.760
We seem to be inching forward
but no real breakthrough

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:25.370
or progress.

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:29.580
And then the General Assembly
met on the 12th of September.

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.310
And at that time,
the US government

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:34.790
had become very focused
on the Iraq issue.

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:38.090
I spoke, and
President Bush spoke.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:41.270
We both demanded Iraq to comply.

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:45.320
In my statement, I
made several point.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:48.800
But there are two I would want
to share with you, arguing

00:27:48.800 --> 00:27:52.220
that any country
when attacked has

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:56.390
an inherent right of
self-defense under Article 51

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:58.400
of the UN Charter.

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:01.400
But when that country decides
to deal with a broader

00:28:01.400 --> 00:28:05.870
threat to international
peace and security,

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:09.890
the approval and support
of the Security Council

00:28:09.890 --> 00:28:13.910
is required, that we need to
have that basic understanding.

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:18.170
Otherwise, we will face major
difficulties down the line.

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:21.140
So we were all delighted
when President Bush

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:24.010
spoke and informed
the world that he

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:28.760
is going to refer the Iraq
issue to the United Nations.

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:32.900
I also indicated to the council
that in such situations,

00:28:32.900 --> 00:28:35.165
they would also have
to have the will

00:28:35.165 --> 00:28:39.020
and live up to the
responsibilities.

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:40.940
The Security Council,
you know what

00:28:40.940 --> 00:28:42.360
has happened in the Congress.

00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:45.720
And so I'm not going
to comment on that.

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:48.440
But in the Security
Council, there

00:28:48.440 --> 00:28:52.910
is a debate going on now
as to the circumstances

00:28:52.910 --> 00:28:56.240
under which the inspectors
should be sent back.

00:28:56.240 --> 00:28:59.420
There is a sense that
the disarmament regime

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:01.040
will need to be strengthened.

00:29:01.040 --> 00:29:03.830
The inspectors would need to be
strengthened so that we don't

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:07.640
repeat the errors of
the past, and they

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:12.110
can go in with a strengthened
hand and get their work done.

00:29:12.110 --> 00:29:14.870
My sense is that that
resolution will be passed.

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:17.210
There's still lots of
discussions and negotiations

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:18.470
going on.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:21.980
And I expect the council
to send in the inspectors

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:25.680
with their strengthened regime
with a demand that Iraq should

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.570
perform and with the request
that the inspectors should

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:33.310
report back should
Iraq fail to do so.

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:36.410
And if Iraq were
to fail to do so,

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:41.090
I suspect the council will
indicate the consequences

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:43.940
that Iraq will
have to face up to.

00:29:43.940 --> 00:29:48.110
There has been a suggestion that
maybe even in this resolution

00:29:48.110 --> 00:29:51.650
there should be the
phrase, "if Iraq fails,

00:29:51.650 --> 00:29:54.410
all necessary means
must be used,"

00:29:54.410 --> 00:29:58.620
which is a code phrase
for military action.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:01.660
But I think the member states
want a two-stage approach.

00:30:01.660 --> 00:30:03.530
Send in the inspectors.

00:30:03.530 --> 00:30:07.130
If they get into trouble,
if it fails, come back,

00:30:07.130 --> 00:30:11.670
and we will pass the
second resolution.

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:12.890
So this is where we are.

00:30:12.890 --> 00:30:17.570
And I expect that within
the next week or two

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:19.670
the council will have acted.

00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:23.730
The Chief Inspector Hans Blix
and his team are ready to go.

00:30:23.730 --> 00:30:26.810
They met with the Iraqis and
discussed the practical aspects

00:30:26.810 --> 00:30:28.730
of resumption of their work.

00:30:28.730 --> 00:30:30.350
But they are waiting
for the council

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:35.420
to act so that if there
are new instructions,

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:39.150
they can factor it
into their work.

00:30:39.150 --> 00:30:43.290
And of course, all the member
states will work together.

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:48.020
And my plea to the council is
that they should work in unity.

00:30:48.020 --> 00:30:50.210
From experience, it is
only when the council

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:54.330
is united that they have
the greatest impact.

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:57.770
And I hope and trust
that the council will

00:30:57.770 --> 00:31:00.530
be able to manage
this process in a way

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:04.250
that we keep the unity of
the member states together.

00:31:04.250 --> 00:31:07.460
We keep the USA in the fold
working with the Security

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:12.110
Council not just for today but
for tomorrow and the day after.

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:13.040
Thank you very much.

00:31:13.040 --> 00:31:14.873
PRESENTER: Thank you,
Mr. Secretary-General.

00:31:14.873 --> 00:31:16.970
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:19.820 --> 00:31:20.570
Thank you so much.

00:31:20.570 --> 00:31:23.020
That was extraordinary.

